\begin{document}

\section{Summary}
Over the past decade, genome-wide association studies (GWAS) have identified hundreds of thousands of genetic variants associated with complex traits and diseases \citep{Sollis:2022}. The majority of these variants are from non-coding regions, suggesting that their effects are mediated through transcriptional 
regulation \citep{Maurano:2012, Nicolae:2010}. This has motivated the need to identify expression quantitative trait loci (eQTLs), genetic variants associated with changes in gene expression, and use them to link GWAS variants to their regulatory targets \citep{GTEx:2020, Gamazon:2018}. However, identification of disease-relevant eQTLs remains challenging for several reasons. The first is that most disease-relevant eQTLs are context-specific, meaning that the variant regulates the same gene's expression differently across cell types and tissues \citep{Umans:2021, Mostafavi:2023, Fujita:2024}. Second, both 
bulk and single-cell RNA-sequencing studies rely on repeated sampling, where the donor provides samples across multiple contexts. This design introduces intra-individual correlation, which, if unaccounted for, limits the detection of eQTLs \citep{GTEx:2020, Yazar:2022}.

There are many existing methods for mapping context-specific eQTLs, but none properly address all challenges. Existing methods either rely on linear mixed models (LMMs) to account for intra-individual correlation across contexts \citep{Strober:2024, Cuomo:2022, Kumasaka:2023}, which are statistically 
rigorous but computationally expensive for large real-world datasets, or they map eQTLs separately in each context and define context-specificity post hoc \citep{GTEx:2020, Fujita:2024, Yazar:2022}, which is quick and computationally cheap but very underpowered, and can produce miscalibrated results.

To address these limitations, we introduce FastGxC \citep{Krockenberger:2025}, a method that efficiently maps context-specific eQTLs by decomposing gene expression into a context-shared component and context-specific components. We then estimate genetic effects on each component using ultra-fast linear regression. This reparameterization is statistically equivalent to an LMM with a genotype by context interaction (LMM-GxC) while reducing computation time from years to minutes. \pkg{FastGxC} is an \proglang{R} package providing a flexible implementation of the FastGxC algorithm for bulk and single-cell RNA-seq data. All analyses in this paper can be reproduced using the same scripts provided in the package's GitHub Wiki at \url{https://github.com/BalliuLab/FastGxC/wiki}.


\section{Statement of Need}
Existing methods for mapping context-specific eQTLs broadly fall into two separate categories, each with its own limitations.

First, linear mixed model (LMM)-based approaches with a genotype by context (GxC) interaction jointly model data across contexts and account for intra-individual correlation with a random intercept per individual. However, they suffer from a large computational burden, especially when applied to large datasets. In datasets with many individuals and contexts, an LMM-GxC model could take tens to upwards of 100 years. 

On the other hand, context-by-context (CxC) approaches map eQTLs separately within each context and define context-specificity post hoc by comparing results across contexts. While computationally efficient, these approaches are systematically underpowered because they ignore intra-individual correlation and rely on ad hoc thresholds for defining context-specificity that can produce miscalibrated results. Furthermore, these approaches lose power by not leveraging information across contexts when identifying eQTLs.

Existing methods for context-specific eQTL mapping face a crucial tradeoff between statistical rigor and computational efficiency. To address this gap, we present FastGxC, an R package providing a flexible implementation of the FastGxC algorithm. FastGxC provides (i) a statistically rigorous and well-calibrated framework for mapping context-specific eQTLs, and (ii) computational efficiency, reducing runtime from years to minutes.

\section{Algorithm}
\pkg{FastGxC} maps context-specific eQTLs in three steps. Let $E_{ijc}$ denote the normalized expression of gene $j$ in individual $i$ and context $c$, where contexts index cell types, tissues, or other cellular states $c \in \{1, \dots, C\}$, and let $G_{is}$ denote the genotype (minor allele dosage) of individual $i$ at SNP $s$. Assume individuals $i = 1, \dots, N$ are unrelated and that the expression has been adjusted for known technical and biological covariates.

\textbf{Step 1: Expression decomposition.} \pkg{FastGxC} decomposes the expression of each gene into an individual-level context-shared component and context-specific deviations. Specifically, for every gene $j$ and individual $i$, we define

\begin{equation}
E_{ic} = E_{..} + \underbrace{(E_{i.} - E_{..})}_{E_i^{\mathrm{sh}}} + \underbrace{(E_{ic} - E_{i.})}_{E_{ic}^{\mathrm{sp}}}
\end{equation}


where $E_{..} = \left(\sum_{i=1}^{I} \sum_{c=1}^{C} E_{ic}\right) / (I \times C)$ is the average expression of the gene, computed over all $I$ individuals and all $C$ contexts, and $E_{i.} = \left(\sum_{c=1}^{C} E_{ic}\right) / C$ is the average expression of the gene for individual $i$, computed over all contexts. Here, $E_{..}$ is a term that is constant across individuals and contexts for each gene, $E_i^{\mathrm{sh}}$ is the context-shared expression component for individual $i$ and is constant across contexts for each gene and individual, and $E_{ic}^{\mathrm{sp}}$ is the context-$c$-specific expression component for individual $i$.

\textbf{Step 2: Genetic effect estimation via linear regression.} \pkg{FastGxC} 
estimates one shared and $C$ context-specific cis-genetic effects by regressing 
the genotypes on each component using ultra-fast implementations of fixed-effect 
linear regression models, i.e.,
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
E_i^{\mathrm{sh}} &= \alpha^{\mathrm{sh}} + \beta^{\mathrm{sh}} G_i + \varepsilon_i^{\mathrm{sh}}, \\[4pt]
E_{i1}^{\mathrm{sp}} &= \alpha_1^{\mathrm{sp}} + \beta_1^{\mathrm{sp}} G_i + \varepsilon_{i1}^{\mathrm{sp}}, \\[2pt]
&\;\;\vdots \\[2pt]
E_{iC}^{\mathrm{sp}} &= \alpha_C^{\mathrm{sp}} + \beta_C^{\mathrm{sp}} G_i + \varepsilon_{iC}^{\mathrm{sp}},
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
where $\alpha^{\mathrm{sh}}, \alpha_1^{\mathrm{sp}}, \dots, \alpha_C^{\mathrm{sp}}$ 
are intercepts, $G_i \in \{0, 1, 2\}$ is the genotype of individual $i$ coded as 
the number of minor alleles, and $\beta^{\mathrm{sh}}, \beta_1^{\mathrm{sp}}, \dots, \beta_C^{\mathrm{sp}}$ 
are the genetic effects on the shared and each of the context-specific expression 
components. Finally, $\varepsilon_i^{\mathrm{sh}}, \varepsilon_{i1}^{\mathrm{sp}}, \dots, \varepsilon_{iC}^{\mathrm{sp}}$ 
each normally distributed residual error with mean zero and variances 
$\sigma_{\mathrm{sh}}^2, \sigma_{\mathrm{sp},1}^2, \dots, \sigma_{\mathrm{sp},C}^2$. 
The total effect in context $c$ can be recovered as $\beta_c^{\mathrm{total}} = 
\beta^{\mathrm{sh}} + \beta_c^{\mathrm{sp}}$. Because the shared and 
context-specific components are orthogonal by construction, the models can be 
fit independently, and each regression is univariate on residualized expression. 
The full scan over all gene-SNP-context triples, therefore, reduces to $C + 1$ 
independent ultra-fast matrix-eQTL passes, implemented in \pkg{FastGxC} via 
\pkg{MatrixEQTL} \citep{Shabalin:2012} or, optionally, GPU-accelerated via 
\pkg{tensorqtl} \citep{TaylorWeiner:2019}. This reparameterization is 
statistically equivalent to the full linear mixed model with a gene-by-context 
interaction (LMM-GxC) but reduces the runtime from years to minutes on 
transcriptome-scale data. The proof for this statistical equivalence can be found in the supplement of the FastGxC paper \cite{Krockenberger:2025}.

\textbf{Step 3: Hierarchical multiple testing correction.} Finally, to account 
for multiple testing across genes, SNPs, and contexts, \pkg{FastGxC} employs 
the hierarchical FDR-controlling procedure implemented in \pkg{TreeQTL} 
\citep{Peterson:2016}. We define a gene-SNP pair as an eQTL if the SNP has a 
significant effect on the shared or any of the specific components of the gene 
expression, i.e., if the global null hypothesis 
$H_0: \beta^{\mathrm{sh}} = \beta_1^{\mathrm{sp}} = \beta_2^{\mathrm{sp}} = \cdots = \beta_C^{\mathrm{sp}} = 0$ 
is rejected. If an eQTL exists, we define a \emph{shared-eQTL} as a variant 
with a statistically significant effect on the shared expression component, 
i.e., if $H_0: \beta^{\mathrm{sh}} = 0$ is rejected, and a 
\emph{context-specific eQTL} as a variant with a statistically significant 
genetic effect on at least one context-specific expression component, i.e., 
if the global null hypothesis 
$H_0: \beta_1^{\mathrm{sp}} = \beta_2^{\mathrm{sp}} = \cdots = \beta_C^{\mathrm{sp}} = 0$ 
is rejected. In addition, we define a \emph{specific-eQTL in context $c$} as 
a variant with a statistically significant genetic effect on the context-$c$-specific 
expression component, i.e., if the marginal null hypothesis 
$H_0: \beta_c^{\mathrm{sp}} = 0$ is rejected. Figure~\ref{sfig:hierarchical_testing} 
illustrates the hypothesis structures used by \pkg{FastGxC}.

\begin{figure}[!htbp]
    \centering

    {\Large \textbf{A}}\\[0.5em]
    \resizebox{\textwidth}{!}{%
    \begin{tikzpicture}[
      node distance=1cm and 0cm,
      every node/.style={draw, rounded corners, minimum width=2.2cm, minimum height=1cm, align=center, fill=white}
    ]
    \node (gene1) {Gene 1};
    \node (snp1) [below=of gene1, xshift=-3cm] {SNP 1 \\ $H_0: \beta^{sh} = \beta^{sp}_1= \beta^{sp}_2 = \ldots= 0$};
    \node (dots_snp) [right=1.5cm of snp1, draw=none, minimum size=0pt] {\LARGE $\dots$};
    \node (snps) [right=-1cm of dots_snp, xshift=2cm] {SNP S};
