Users may alternatively apply \pkg{treeBH} \citep{Bogomolov:2020} to the output of Step 2, which 
implements a four-level hierarchy (genes $\to$ SNPs $\to$ shared/specific 
$\to$ contexts) that jointly adjusts for both shared and context-specific 
effects simultaneously. This approach is more conservative and computationally 
intensive, and is recommended only for datasets with a small number of tests. 
Users may also apply \pkg{qvalue} \citep{Storey:2003} to construct custom 
multiple-testing pipelines.

\section{Usage}
\subsection{Installation and Dependencies}
The FastGxC package and its dependencies are available on GitHub and can be installed in R.

First, please install these required dependencies:
\begin{CodeChunk}
<<eval=FALSE>>=
if (!requireNamespace("qvalue", quietly = TRUE)) {
  if (!require("BiocManager", quietly = TRUE)) 
    install.packages("BiocManager")
  BiocManager::install("qvalue")
}
install.packages(
  "http://bioinformatics.org/treeqtl/TreeQTL_2.0.tar.gz",
  repos = NULL)
@
\end{CodeChunk}

Next, FastGxC can be directly installed using the devtools package in R:
\begin{CodeChunk}
<<eval=FALSE>>=
if (!requireNamespace("devtools", quietly = TRUE)) {
  install.packages("devtools")
}
devtools::install_github("BalliuLab/FastGxC")
library(FastGxC)
@
\end{CodeChunk}

Moreover, FastGxC supports Python integration (tensorqtl) for faster eQTL mapping. To enable this functionality, please ensure that Python is installed. Python version 3.7 or higher is required. 

To check your Python setup, run the following command in your terminal:
\begin{CodeChunk}
<<engine="bash", eval=FALSE>>=
which python3
@
\end{CodeChunk}

To install the required Python packages, please paste the following command into your terminal:
\begin{CodeChunk}
<<engine="bash", eval=FALSE>>=
/your/python/path/here -m pip install \
  numpy pandas pyarrow rpy2 torch tensorqtl
@
\end{CodeChunk}

Next, start an R session and set the Python path:
\begin{CodeChunk}
<<eval=FALSE>>=
use_python("/your/python/path/here", required = TRUE)
py <- import_builtins()
@
\end{CodeChunk}

\subsection{Simulate Data}
FastGxC provides a built-in data simulator to generate synthetic datasets in the input format required by the package. The simulator enables (i) verification of a successful installation, (ii) validation of input/output schemas and pipeline wiring, and (iii) controlled end-to-end testing before analysis of empirical data. Users may specify sample size, numbers of genes and variants, context structure, allele frequencies, heritability, missingness, and residual correlation to reproduce a range of scenarios. For reproducibility, a random seed can be set before the simulation. 

\code{simulate\_data()} generates synthetic genotype and gene expression data in user-defined contexts (e.g., cell types or tissues) in the exact input format required by FastGxC. Make sure that the data\_dir\_sim is writable; it will be created if absent. Please visit the GitHub Wiki for more information about its parameters and usage.
\begin{CodeChunk}
<<eval=FALSE>>=
data_dir_sim <- "simulated_example/"
n_contexts   <- 10
simulate_data(
  data_dir        = data_dir_sim,
  N               = 300,
  n_genes         = 100,
  n_snps_per_gene = 1000,
  n_contexts      = n_contexts,
  maf             = 0.2,
  w_corr          = 0.2,
  missing         = 0.05,
  hsq             = c(rep(0, n_contexts-1), 0.2),
  mus             = rep(0, n_contexts),
  cisDist         = 1e6,
  seed            = 1
)
@
\end{CodeChunk}

The function writes four tab-delimited files to data\_dir\_sim. Unless noted, missing values will be encoded as NA. 

\code{SNPs.txt} --- Genotype matrix (SNPs $\times$ individuals). 
Rows index SNPs; columns index individuals; entries are copies of 
minor alleles (0/1/2) or NA.

\begin{CodeChunk}
<<echo=FALSE, eval=FALSE>>=

snpid  ind1  ind2  ind3  ind4  ind5  ...
snp1      0     2     1    NA     0  ...
snp2      1     2    NA     1     1  ...
snp3      0     1     0     1     1  ...
snp4      1    NA     0     1     1  ...
snp5      0     0     0     1     1  ...
@
\end{CodeChunk}

\code{snpsloc.txt} --- SNP annotations and context mask.
Contains snpid, chromosome, position, followed by one column per context. Context columns use 1/0 to indicate whether the SNP is tested in that context.

\begin{CodeChunk}
<<echo=FALSE, eval=FALSE>>=

snpid   chr   pos   context1  context2  ...  context10
snp1    chr1   1        1         1           1
snp2    chr1   1        1         1           0 
snp3    chr1   1        1         0           1
snp4    chr1   1        0         1           1
snp5    chr1   1        1         1           1
@
\end{CodeChunk}

\code{expression.txt} --- Expression matrix (design $\times$ genes)
Each row corresponds to an individual–context pairing in the design column, followed by one column per gene.

\begin{CodeChunk}
<<echo=FALSE, eval=FALSE>>=

design            gene1     gene2      gene3    ...   gene100
ind1 - context1   0.4369    NA         -1.8113  ...   1.1721
ind1 - context10  0.6437    -0.4357    -0.8296  ...  -0.3944
ind1 - context2   0.1092    -0.1294    -0.3222  ...  -0.4939
ind1 - context3   NA        -0.1947    -0.1111  ...  -0.9999
ind1 - context4   0.8347    -0.9876    NA       ...  -0.3833
@
\end{CodeChunk}

\code{geneloc.txt} --- Gene annotations and context mask
Gene location file with geneid, chromosome, start (s1) and end (s2) positions of the gene, followed by one column per context. Context columns use 1/0 to indicate whether the gene is tested in that context.

\begin{CodeChunk}
<<echo=FALSE, eval=FALSE>>=

geneid  chr   s1          s2          context1  context2  context3  context4
gene1   chr1  0           1001            1         1         1         1
gene2   chr1  100000001   100001001       1         1         1         1
gene3   chr1  200000001   200001001       1         1         1         1
gene4   chr1  300000001   300001001       1         1         0         1
gene5   chr1  400000001   400001001       1         1         1         0
gene6   chr1  500000001   500001001       1         1         1         1
@
\end{CodeChunk}

\subsection{Decomposition Step}
The decomposition\_step() function decomposes the input gene expression data across C contexts (e.g., tissues, cell types, or experimental conditions) into one shared and C context-specific components. By removing variation that is common across contexts, this step increases the power to detect context-specific eQTLs.
\begin{CodeChunk}
<<echo=FALSE, eval=FALSE>>=

data_dir_decomp <- "simulated_example/"
exp_mat_filename <- paste0(data_dir_decomp, "expression.txt")

decomposition_step(exp_mat_filename, data_dir_decomp)
@
\end{CodeChunk}

FastGxC natively supports missing expression values; imputation is not required. Before decomposition, ensure that all missing entries in the expression matrix are encoded as NA. This allows the model to correctly account for incomplete observations during fitting. 

Moreover, FastGxC assumes an approximately Gaussian expression matrix. To ensure that your data fits these assumptions, you should perform a variance-stabilizing transformation (e.g., log2(TPM + 1)) or, preferably, quantile normalization before running FastGxC.

In addition, to improve eQTL mapping power, we suggest residualizing the expression matrix for technical and biological covariates before running FastGxC. Common approaches include:

- Regressing out measured factors (e.g., batch, sequencing depth, sex, age, or other known confounders).

- Latent factor adjustment, such as PEER factors or PCA-derived covariates, to capture unmeasured sources of variation. We suggest using PCA-derived covariates.

These preprocessing steps help ensure that the decomposition focuses on biologically meaningful variation rather than technical artifacts.

The decomposition\_step() function expects an input expression matrix formatted with genes as columns and samples as rows. The first column must be labeled design and contain unique sample identifiers in the format "individual ID - context name" (e.g., ind1 - context3). Consistent naming is critical — mismatches in sample identifiers between the expression matrix and genotype data will cause errors in downstream steps.
\begin{CodeChunk}
<<echo=FALSE, eval=FALSE>>=

design             gene1     gene2     gene3    ...   gene100
ind1 - context1   0.4369    -1.7087    -1.8113  ...   1.1721
ind1 - context10  0.6437    -0.4357    -0.8296  ...  -0.3944
ind1 - context2   0.1092    -0.1294    -0.3222  ...  -0.4939
ind1 - context3   0.1234    -0.1947    -0.1111  ...  -0.9999
ind1 - context4   0.8347    -0.9876    -0.1490  ...  -0.3833
@
\end{CodeChunk}

For C contexts, the decomposition\_step() function outputs C+1 files, one with the shared expression component (i.e., average expression of each gene and individual across context) and C with the specific expression component in each context (i.e., residual expression of each gene and individual in context after subtracting the shared component). Columns in each file correspond to individuals, and rows correspond to genes. The shared file looks identical to the C-specific files:
\begin{CodeChunk}
<<echo=FALSE, eval=FALSE>>=

        ind1        ind2        ind3        ind4        ind5
gene1  0.4321     -1.2345      0.5678     -0.8910      1.1203
gene2 -0.9834      0.1342     -1.0923      0.2345     -0.7583
gene3  1.2948      1.1023      0.8421      0.9104      1.0012
gene4 -0.1243     -0.8934     -1.2098     -1.1342     -0.9981
gene5  0.4587      0.5632      0.9983      1.2345      0.8231
@
\end{CodeChunk}

\subsection{eQTL Mapping}
FastGxC estimates genetic effects on the context-shared and each context-specific component using ultra-fast linear regression models. It uses the MatrixEQTL R package to perform cis-eQTL mapping using the eQTL\_mapping\_step() function. This function takes input genotypes, expression, and gene/SNP location files (see simulate data and decomposition sections for format of input files), and produces summary statistics in MatrixEQTL format. Below is an example code demonstration of how to run eQTL mapping on the shared and C-specific expression components from the simulated data:
\begin{CodeChunk}
<<eval=FALSE>>=
input_dir <- "simulated_example/"
out_dir <- input_dir
expr_files <- list.files(input_dir,
  pattern = "_specific_expression.txt$")
nC <- length(expr_files)
context_names <- paste0("context", seq(1, nC))
all_contexts <- c(context_names, "shared")
SNP_file_name <- file.path(input_dir, "SNPs.txt")
snps_location_file_name <- file.path(input_dir, "snpsloc.txt")
gene_location_file_name <- file.path(input_dir, "geneloc.txt")
for (i in seq_along(all_contexts)) {
  context <- all_contexts[i]
  if (context == "shared") {
    shared_specific <- "shared"
    expression_file_name <- file.path(input_dir, 
      "context_shared_expression.txt")
  } else {
    shared_specific <- "specific"
    expression_file_name <- file.path(input_dir,
      paste0(context, "_specific_expression.txt"))
  }
  eQTL_mapping_step(
    SNP_file_name           = SNP_file_name,
    snps_location_file_name = snps_location_file_name,
    expression_file_name    = expression_file_name,
    gene_location_file_name = gene_location_file_name,
    context                 = context,
    out_dir                 = out_dir,
    output_file_name_cis    = file.path(out_dir,
      paste0(context, "_", shared_specific, ".cis_pairs.txt")),
    output_file_name_tra    = file.path(out_dir,
      paste0(context, "_", shared_specific, ".trans_pairs.txt")),
    use_model               = modelLINEAR,
    cis_dist                = 1e6,
    pv_threshold_cis        = 1,
    pv_threshold_tra        = 0,
    error_covariance        = numeric()
  )
}
@
\end{CodeChunk}

FastGxC also supports GPU-accelerated cis-eQTL mapping using tensorqtl, integrated via the reticulate R package. To enable this, simply pass method = "tensorqtl" to the eQTL\_mapping\_step() function. The inputs remain unchanged. This method is particularly useful for large datasets and provides substantial speedups. Please note that tensorQTL requires Python 3.7+ and appropriate libraries (tensorqtl, numpy, pandas, pyarrow, torch). Ensure your environment is properly configured.

FastGxC will save C+1 summary statistics files in .txt format (see the output file example below) for compatibility with downstream FastGxC multiple testing adjustment steps:
\begin{CodeChunk}
<<echo=FALSE, eval=FALSE>>=

SNP       gene    beta      p.value    FDR
snp64928  gene65   0.4187   8.48e-07   0.0848
snp42566  gene43  -0.3558   7.53e-06   0.3068
snp22672  gene23  -0.3948   9.20e-06   0.3068
snp73350  gene74   0.3445   1.60e-05   0.3998
snp86064  gene87  -0.3715   3.10e-05   0.5505
@
\end{CodeChunk}

FastGxC decomposes eQTL effects into a shared component and context-specific 
deviations. By construction, specific effects represent deviations from the 
mean effect across contexts. Positive values indicate stronger-than-average 
effects; negative values indicate weaker-than-average effects. The full eQTL 
effect size in each context $\beta_{c}$ can always be recovered by adding the 
shared $\beta^{sh}$ and specific effects $\beta^{sp}_{c}$, i.e. $\beta_{c} = 
\beta^{sh} + \beta^{sp}_{c}$.

\begin{CodeChunk}
<<eval=FALSE>>=
input_dir <- "simulated_example/"
shared <- read.table(
  file.path(input_dir, "shared_shared.cis_pairs.txt"),
  header = TRUE, stringsAsFactors = FALSE)
specific_files <- list.files(input_dir,
  pattern = "_specific\\.cis_pairs\\.txt$",
  full.names = TRUE)
total_effects <- lapply(specific_files, function(f) {
  context <- gsub("_specific\\.cis_pairs\\.txt$", "", basename(f))
  specific <- read.table(f, header = TRUE, stringsAsFactors = FALSE)
  merged <- merge(shared, specific, by = c("SNP", "gene"),
    suffixes = c(".sh", ".sp"))
  merged$beta.total <- merged$beta.sh + merged$beta.sp
  merged$context <- context
  merged[, c("SNP", "gene", "context",
    "beta.sh", "beta.sp", "beta.total")]
})
total_effects <- do.call(rbind, total_effects)
head(total_effects)
@
\end{CodeChunk}

\subsection{Multiple Testing Correction}
FastGxC accounts for multiple hypothesis testing across genes, SNPs, and biological contexts using a hierarchical false discovery rate (FDR) controlling procedure. This step ensures proper correction at multiple levels (e.g., eGenes). 

FastGxC leverages the TreeQTL R package to perform hierarchical multiple testing correction. The treeQTL\_step() function identifies both context-specific and shared eGenes and eAssociations by controlling FDR across a structured hypothesis space.

TreeQTL requires input files formatted according to MatrixEQTL output conventions with p-values in increasing order (from smallest to largest). If you used alternative software like FastQTL, ensure the outputs are formatted correctly.

You can optionally use other multiple testing frameworks, such as mashR, which may yield higher power under some assumptions.

Please note that this function expects files to be named in the same way as the output files from FastGxC's eQTL mapping function.
\begin{CodeChunk}
<<eval=FALSE>>=
data_dir <- "simulated_example/"
out_dir <- data_dir
snps_location_file_name <- file.path(data_dir, "snpsloc.txt")
gene_location_file_name <- file.path(data_dir, "geneloc.txt")
fdr_thresh <- 0.05
cisDist <- 1e6
@
\end{CodeChunk}

The first option is to perform separate multiple testing adjustments for shared (two-level hierarchy) and specific (three-level hierarchy) effects. This is the strategy used in real data analysis in the manuscript. This will return files with shared and specific eGenes and eAssociations.
\begin{CodeChunk}
<<eval=FALSE>>=
treeQTL_step(
  data_dir                = data_dir,
  snps_location_file_name = snps_location_file_name,
  gene_location_file_name = gene_location_file_name,
  context_names           = context_names,
  out_dir                 = out_dir,
  cisDist                 = cisDist,
  fdr_thresh              = fdr_thresh,
  four_level              = FALSE,
  qtl_type                = "cis"
)
@
\end{CodeChunk}

treeQTL outputs (i) An eAssociation file per context with context-specific SNP-gene pairs. (ii) A shared eGenes file with each shared eGene and its corresponding p-value. (iii) A specific eGene file with a 1 corresponding to the gene being a significant eGene in that particular context. A specific eGene p-value file reporting all p-value combinations for each significant eGene across all contexts.

(i) eAssoc\_by\_gene.contextX\_specific\_cis.txt
\begin{CodeChunk}
<<echo=FALSE, eval=FALSE>>=

gene     SNP         beta                t.stat                 p.value
gene1   snp1    0.368806164512139    5.22116640681629    3.34162013627681e-07
gene2   snp2    0.423778936498137    5.67707930801708    3.25067273366269e-08
gene3   snp3    0.437418111526831    4.98770927957996    1.03954367213838e-06
gene4   snp4    0.462203944759481    5.36767905554521    1.60616150168334e-07
gene5   snp5    0.5495476161726      6.75612550096954    7.44084554530075e-11
@
\end{CodeChunk}

(ii) shared\_eGenes.txt
\begin{CodeChunk}
<<echo=FALSE, eval=FALSE>>=

family         fam_p           n_sel
gene1   1.40293481281949e-09    1
gene2   1.14061108736042e-14    1
gene3   3.35665396944856e-12    1
gene4   3.03128672087492e-13    1
gene5   3.55458571779909e-11    2
@
\end{CodeChunk}

(iii) specific\_eGenes.txt
\begin{CodeChunk}
<<echo=FALSE, eval=FALSE>>=

gene    context1    context2   context3   context4   context5        
gene1     0            0           0          0          0       
gene2     1            0           0          0          0      
gene3     0            0           0          0          0 
gene4     0            0           0          0          0 
gene5     0            0           0          0          0  
@
\end{CodeChunk}

(iii) specific\_eGenes\_pvalues.txt
\begin{CodeChunk}
<<echo=FALSE, eval=FALSE>>=

gene         context1                context2                context3
gene1     0.623725805802154       0.62987552431814        0.888515702826435
gene2     5.25935519127471e-05    0.571468511077802       0.33413356471655
gene3     0.634499598043283       0.725117041047635       0.148461103453072
gene4     0.59324391608897        0.44380106630737        0.378896498929132
gene5     0.0929487562865985      0.756063879067856       0.54373880161688
@
\end{CodeChunk}

The second option is to perform multiple testing adjustments across shared and specific effects using the four-level hierarchy shown below. Note that this strategy uses TreeBH in its implementation and is computationally intensive and should only be used in settings with a small number of tests performed.
\begin{CodeChunk}
<<eval=FALSE>>=
treeQTL_step(
  data_dir                = data_dir,
  snps_location_file_name = snps_location_file_name,
  gene_location_file_name = gene_location_file_name,
  context_names           = context_names,
  out_dir                 = out_dir,
  cisDist                 = cisDist,
  fdr_thresh              = fdr_thresh,
  four_level              = TRUE,
  qtl_type                = "cis"
)
@
\end{CodeChunk}

treeBH outputs a file of gene, SNP, context triplets denoting the significant pairs identified after multiple testing correction.s
\begin{CodeChunk}
<<echo=FALSE, eval=FALSE>>=

gene_name     snp_name    context_name
gene1           snp1       context1
gene2           snp2       context2
gene3           snp3       context2
gene4           snp4       context3
gene5           snp5       context4
@
\end{CodeChunk}


\section{Reproducibility and Dependencies}
All examples in this paper can be reproduced using the scripts provided in the package's GitHub Wiki at \url{https://github.com/BalliuLab/FastGxC/wiki}. The analyses were performed under \proglang{R} version 4.3.2 with \pkg{FastGxC} version 1.0.0. The package depends on \pkg{MatrixEQTL} version 2.3 \citep{Shabalin:2012} (with \pkg{tensorqtl} version 1.0.8 \citep{TaylorWeiner:2019} as an alternative GPU-accelerated option) for eQTL mapping, and \pkg{TreeQTL} version 2.0 \citep{Peterson:2016} (with \pkg{treeBH} as an alternative option) for hierarchical multiple testing correction. Additional dependencies include \pkg{qvalue} version 2.34.0 \citep{Storey:2003}, \pkg{devtools} version 2.4.5, and standard tidyverse packages (\pkg{dplyr} $\geq$ 1.1.0, \pkg{tidyr} $\geq$ 1.3.0, \pkg{data.table} $\geq$ 1.14.0). Note that \pkg{qvalue} and \pkg{TreeQTL} require manual installation as described in the Installation and Dependencies subsection under the Usage section. All remaining dependencies are automatically installed and loaded upon installation of the \pkg{FastGxC} package. Optional Python integration via \pkg{tensorqtl} requires \proglang{Python} $\geq$ 3.7 with \pkg{numpy}, \pkg{pandas}, \pkg{pyarrow}, \pkg{rpy2}, and \pkg{torch}. The package passes all standard \code{R CMD check} tests without errors or warnings.

\section{Implementation and Future Work}
There are possibilities to extend \pkg{FastGxC} to increase its power and applicability. The current implementation assumes a single shared component across all contexts, which may not capture finer subgroup structures such as brain tissues in GTEx \citep{GTEx:2020}. Incorporating hierarchical decompositions could improve estimates of context-group-specific eQTL effects and context-specific eQTL effects. Additionally, the current decomposition defines context-specificity as deviation from the average effect across all contexts; adjusting the method for baseline-context comparisons would expand its applicability to time-course and environmental perturbation experiments. Further improvements could come from extending \pkg{FastGxC} to handle non-normal phenotypes using a generalized linear model, or from integrating fine-mapping approaches such as SuSiE \citep{Wang:2020} to refine candidate causal variants at significant loci. Finally, integration with single-cell-aware context-specific eQTL methods such as CellRegMap \citep{CuomoCellRegMap:2022} and SAIGE-QTL \citep{Zhou:2022} would enable direct benchmarking and hybrid workflows on large-scale single-cell RNA-seq datasets.

